{
  "title": "Texture pipeline: decode -> composite -> render",
  "description": "How a Doom texture goes from raw bytes in a WAD file to a textured wall or floor in the 3D view - explained from the ground up, including Doom's own file formats, not just this codebase's code.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "description": "### From WAD bytes to a textured wall\n\nThis tour follows one texture's entire journey through the app: raw bytes inside a WAD file -> decoded pixels -> a composited texture -> a cached Godot material -> UV coordinates on an actual 3D mesh.\n\nDoom's own file formats are 30+ years old and were designed to be as small as possible on 1993 hardware, so several steps along the way look stranger than they'd be if this were built from scratch today. This tour explains those formats as it goes - you shouldn't need any prior knowledge of Doom internals or of Ultimate Doom Builder (UDB), the reference editor this project's map/texture logic is deliberately ported from, to follow along."
    },
    {
      "description": "### The big picture, before any code\n\nA `.wad` file is just a flat container of named binary blobs called \"lumps\" - think of it like a zip file with no folders, just a list of `(name, bytes)` pairs. `DOOM2.WAD` might have thousands of these: one per map, one per sound, one per each small image, etc.\n\nA texture you see on a wall in-game is actually assembled from several different kinds of lumps working together:\n\n1. **`PLAYPAL`** - a single lookup table of 256 colors (a \"palette\"). Doom images don't store colors directly; every pixel is just a number 0-255 saying *which* of these 256 colors to use. This was a real memory-saving trick in 1993 (1 byte per pixel instead of 3+).\n2. **Patches** - small images (used for wall art and monster/item sprites), stored in a special format that skips fully-transparent pixels entirely rather than wasting space storing them.\n3. **Flats** - floor/ceiling images. Simpler than patches, since floors and ceilings are always fully-opaque rectangles - no transparency to encode.\n4. **`PNAMES` + `TEXTURE1`/`TEXTURE2`** - most *wall* textures aren't a single image at all. They're \"recipes\" that composite several small patches together at specific positions, like a collage, onto one bigger final texture. This is why a texture name in a map might not be a lump you can find directly.\n\nThe rest of this tour walks through the code that implements each of these, in that order, then follows the result out into the actual 3D rendering."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/DoomArchitect.Core/Textures/Playpal.cs",
      "line": 29,
      "description": "### Step 1: the palette\n\nThis is `PLAYPAL` in code: 256 colors, each stored as 3 bytes (red, green, blue), read straight off disk. Every other decoder in this pipeline (patches, flats) reads a byte 0-255 out of the image data and looks it up here to get an actual color - that's what \"indexed color\" means in practice: `Playpal.cs` *is* the lookup table. If no `PLAYPAL` lump is found at all (a malformed or unusual WAD), this falls back to a flat gray palette rather than crashing."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/DoomArchitect.Core/Textures/DoomPictureReader.cs",
      "line": 77,
      "description": "### Step 2: patches, and why they're stored as \"posts\"\n\nA patch (used for wall art and sprites) is stored one column of pixels at a time. Instead of storing every pixel top-to-bottom - including the fully see-through gaps around an irregularly-shaped monster or torch - each column is a short list of \"posts\": a post says \"starting at row N, here are M solid pixels\", and gaps between posts are simply skipped (they render as transparent). This is a simple form of run-length encoding built specifically around transparency.\n\nEach post's starting row (\"topdelta\") is stored in a single byte, so it can normally only be 0-254 (255 is reserved to mean \"end of this column\"). That's a real problem for a patch taller than 254 pixels - some posts can't express their true row number in one byte. The line highlighted here is the historical workaround: if a post's topdelta looks like it's going backwards (or, specifically for patches over 256px tall, exactly repeats the previous value), it's treated as *added onto* the previous position instead of read as an absolute row - letting positions beyond 254 be built up from smaller pieces. UDB's own code comment claims this exists for patches \"higher than 508 pixels\", but the actual check is `height > 256` - the real code, not the (apparently stale) comment, is what got reproduced here."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/DoomArchitect.Core/Textures/DoomFlatReader.cs",
      "line": 24,
      "description": "### Step 3: flats are much simpler - almost\n\nA flat (floor/ceiling texture) has no header, no posts, no transparency handling at all - just a flat grid of palette-index bytes, conventionally 64x64 (4096 bytes). Since there's no header, the *size* has to be figured out from how many bytes the lump actually contains.\n\nThe code here does that: if the byte count is a perfect square (64x64, 128x128, etc.), it uses that as the width/height. But for a malformed lump that's neither a perfect square nor exactly 4096 bytes, yet still bigger than 4096, it's forced to 64x64 and only the first 4096 bytes are read - silently throwing away the rest. That's a real quirk in the original editor's handling of bad data, reproduced here deliberately rather than \"fixed\", since matching how the reference editor actually behaves matters more here than being extra permissive."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/DoomArchitect.Core/Textures/TextureDefinitionReader.cs",
      "line": 7,
      "description": "### Step 4: most wall textures are collages, not single images\n\nHere's where the \"composite texture\" idea from the big-picture step becomes code. `PNAMES` is a master list of every patch name available to be used as a building block. `TEXTURE1`/`TEXTURE2` are lists of \"recipes\": a texture name, its final width/height, and a list of `(patch name, x offset, y offset)` entries saying which small patches to stamp onto the final canvas and where. This is what lets id Software reuse the same small patch images (a brick pattern, a stripe, a logo) across dozens of differently-named final textures instead of storing every texture as one big unique image.\n\nThis reader also makes two deliberate changes from how the reference editor's code actually works, agreed on before writing this:\n\n- The reference editor reads a table of byte-offsets for each recipe but never actually uses them to jump to the right spot - it just assumes recipes are laid out back-to-back in order (true for every real file in practice, but not something the format guarantees). This code uses the real stored offsets instead (`stream.Seek(offsets[i], ...)`, line 51) - same result on every real file, but correct even on a theoretical file that isn't laid out that way.\n- The reference editor's per-recipe sanity check has a copy-paste-looking bug: `(width>0 && height>0 && patches>0 && scalex!=0) || scaley!=0` - because of how `||` and `&&` combine, that `||` makes the whole check pass almost no matter what. This code uses the version that was clearly intended - all four conditions joined with `&&` (line 89)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/DoomArchitect.Core/Textures/CompositeTextureBuilder.cs",
      "line": 93,
      "description": "### Step 5: actually building the collage - and a bug I found here\n\n\"Compositing\" just means: start with a blank canvas, and draw each patch onto it at its recorded offset, later patches drawn on top of earlier ones (so, e.g., a patch with a door-frame cutout drawn over a patch of plain wall produces a wall with a doorway in it).\n\nA patch pixel is either fully solid or fully see-through (see Step 2) - so \"is this pixel see-through\" is a yes/no question, and this method needs to answer it twice: once while drawing, and once while checking whether a whole patch counts as \"masked\" (has any see-through pixel anywhere). The reference editor's actual check is `pixel.alpha > 0.5`, compared against a raw 0-255 byte value - and because of how that comparison works, *any* non-zero alpha value (1 through 255) counts as \"solid\", and only exactly 0 counts as \"see-through\". It's really an \"is this exactly zero\" check wearing a \"more than half\" costume.\n\nAn earlier version of this code used a more intuitive-looking `alpha > 127` (an actual halfway split) instead - which matches the reference editor perfectly for ordinary Doom patches (their pixels are always fully 0 or fully 255 anyway), but silently disagreed with it for a PNG-based patch with genuine partial transparency (e.g. 50% see-through), where the reference editor treats that as fully solid and this early version would have treated it as see-through. Caught in review, fixed to match exactly (see the `IsMasked` method's doc comment for the full explanation), and locked in with a regression test so it can't quietly regress again."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/DoomArchitect.Core/Geometry/LinedefWallBuilder.cs",
      "line": 144,
      "description": "### Step 6: why this whole pipeline needed to exist\n\nA quick bit of Doom mapping vocabulary: a wall segment between two rooms (a \"two-sided linedef\") can have an upper part, a lower part, and a *middle* part. Normally the middle part is empty (you can just see through into the next room). But it can optionally hold a \"masked middle texture\" - something like a chain-link fence, window bars, or prison bars - a see-through image that hangs in that gap without blocking it off like a solid wall would.\n\nSizing that texture correctly requires knowing its actual pixel height, which is exactly the piece of information this whole texture pipeline exists to provide - this feature was explicitly left unimplemented until now for exactly that reason. The method here anchors the texture's top edge to the top of the gap between the two rooms and lets it hang down by its own real height, clipped if it's taller than the gap - matching the reference editor's own default placement behavior."
    },
    {
      "file": "Scripts/Rendering/WallMeshBuilder.cs",
      "line": 48,
      "description": "### Step 7: handing pixels off to Godot - one texture per mesh \"surface\"\n\nFrom here on we're in the Godot-specific half of the app (everything before this point works without Godot even running, which is why it could be so thoroughly unit-tested). A 3D mesh in Godot is built from one or more \"surfaces\", and each surface can only be painted with a single material/texture - you can't paint half a surface with one texture and half with another.\n\nThe problem: a single wall segment's upper, lower, and middle parts (Step 6) can each need a *different* texture. So this code groups a wall's pieces by which texture they use, and builds one surface per group (`SurfaceTool.Commit(mesh)` appends a new surface rather than replacing the mesh) instead of dumping everything into one surface. `MapView` (Step 9) can then paint each surface with its own correct material."
    },
    {
      "file": "Scripts/Rendering/TextureCache.cs",
      "line": 65,
      "description": "### Step 8: from raw pixels to something Godot can actually draw\n\nThis is where a decoded `PixelImage` (plain width/height/bytes, nothing Godot-specific) finally becomes a real Godot `Image`/texture wrapped in a `StandardMaterial3D`, cached so the same texture name isn't re-decoded and re-uploaded every time it's needed.\n\nOne setting here is worth calling out because it's the *only* deliberately non-authentic choice in this whole pipeline: `TextureFilter = Nearest`. By default, Godot smooths textures when they're viewed up close or at an angle - normally desirable, but Doom's art is intentionally low-resolution and chunky, and smoothing it would blur away that look. Nearest-neighbor filtering keeps each pixel sharp-edged instead. Everywhere else in this pipeline, matching the reference editor's actual behavior was the goal; here, it deliberately wasn't - this is a rendering/visual-style choice, not a data-correctness one."
    },
    {
      "file": "Scripts/View/MapView.cs",
      "line": 177,
      "description": "### Step 9: tying it together - and what a bare \"-\" means\n\nThis is where a mesh instance in the actual running scene finally gets a real material assigned to it, using the texture name/size lookups built up over the whole tour.\n\nOne more piece of Doom-map vocabulary: a linedef's upper/lower/middle texture fields, and a sector's floor/ceiling texture fields, use the literal string `\"-\"` to mean \"deliberately no texture here\" - it's valid, ordinary data, not a mistake. That's different from a texture name that's *set* but can't actually be found (a genuine problem, worth flagging visually with a placeholder checkerboard - see `TextureSet`'s handling of unknown names). The code here treats those two cases differently on purpose: a `\"-\"` surface just gets no material at all, left at Godot's own plain default, while an unresolvable name would show the loud placeholder instead."
    }
  ]
}
